1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,667
I listen to English podcasts before breakfast while the
rest of the house is still quiet.

2
00:00:06,917 --> 00:00:13,167
My brother tries to want to watch every lesson, but his
schedule rarely permits that.

3
00:00:13,417 --> 00:00:20,083
We memorize vocabulary using short stories instead of
copying isolated words into a notebook every evening.

4
00:00:20,333 --> 00:00:27,417
A clear example helps us acquire language naturally,
although practice still requires attention and a little
patience.

5
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:34,750
Our teacher says that comprehensible input should be
understandable without translating every unfamiliar word
in a sentence.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,083
You can recognize patterns more easily when familiar
expressions appear in several different conversations
during the week.

7
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:50,250
That method makes learning English less intimidating for
students who previously struggled to understand ordinary
conversations outside the classroom.

8
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:57,583
Please forget any complex language learning plans that
promise perfect results without regular listening or
meaningful conversation.

9
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:04,500
The teacher asks us to have a go even when our
pronunciation is still rather uncertain.

10
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:11,833
I picked up several useful expressions from the story
without consulting a dictionary after every single
sentence.

11
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:18,750
We listen to one recording over and over again until its
rhythm begins to sound familiar.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,083
There is a lot of English in my neighborhood, especially
when tourists arrive during the summer holidays.

13
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:33,417
Our instructor recommends that we start with simple
material rather than an unfamiliar technical lecture
about mathematics.

14
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:40,750
I hope to become fluent eventually, but today I only
need to explain my train journey clearly.

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,667
We use English on a daily basis at the workshop, even
during short conversations about lunch.

16
00:01:47,917 --> 00:01:55,000
Everyone should sit down before the recording begins,
because moving chairs make the dialogue difficult to
hear.

17
00:01:55,250 --> 00:02:02,750
We need at least ten minutes of conversation, and the
quieter students should have enough time to respond.

18
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,833
The students help each other with pronunciation and
share their own examples as well.

19
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:16,167
I practice in my spare time after dinner, when nobody
needs the kitchen table for anything else.

20
00:02:16,417 --> 00:02:23,917
Our teacher smiles and says see you tomorrow before
closing the classroom door and carrying her books
downstairs.

21
00:02:24,167 --> 00:02:31,250
Yesterday I learned to acquire language through stories,
and today I want to test that approach independently.

22
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:38,167
My cousin likes to watch it in English, although the
film itself is originally from France.

23
00:02:38,417 --> 00:02:45,500
I do it because I enjoy the exercise, rather than
because anyone promised me an examination certificate.

24
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:52,833
The introduction seems really simple, but the speaker
gradually includes longer sentences and less familiar
vocabulary later.

25
00:02:53,083 --> 00:03:00,167
This activity should be easy enough for the new
students, provided the instructions remain clear and
short.

26
00:03:00,417 --> 00:03:07,500
Some students get input from radio programs, while
others prefer quiet reading because they dislike
background music.

27
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:15,250
I listen to the speaker carefully, even when the
recording includes unfamiliar names and numbers near the
beginning.

28
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:23,000
We need to listen and listen and listen to hundreds of
hours of understandable English over several years.

29
00:03:23,250 --> 00:03:29,500
I over and over again repeated the sentence, then
repeated it over and over again.

30
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:36,833
There are a lot of examples in this chapter and a lot of
explanations in the appendix.

31
00:03:37,083 --> 00:03:44,167
I became fluent gradually while living abroad, rather
than suddenly after watching a single exciting video
online.

32
00:03:44,417 --> 00:03:51,917
She picked it up quickly when the local children
demonstrated the game without translating any of the
instructions.

33
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:59,667
He picks the small notebook up from the desk and returns
it to its owner after the lesson.

34
00:03:59,917 --> 00:04:07,000
The tutor says see you tomorrow at the workshop while
waving goodbye to everyone from the entrance.

35
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:14,750
From this balcony I can see you standing beside the
fountain, although your face is difficult to recognize.

36
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The same students recognized patterns in the new story
after comparing it with material from the previous week.

37
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:30,250
We memorized vocabulary before the exam, but the teacher
also required us to use every word in context.

38
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:37,583
My sister works on a daily basis with visitors who need
directions to nearby museums and restaurants.

39
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:45,333
The recording contains ordinary descriptions of a wooden
shelf, three green chairs, and a window facing the
garden.

40
00:04:45,583 --> 00:04:53,500
The worksheet lists seven numbers in ascending order and
asks students to copy them into the empty boxes below.

41
00:04:53,750 --> 00:05:01,667
Our small office is preparing a community event, and
today the team finally meets to discuss the arrangements
together.

42
00:05:01,917 --> 00:05:09,000
We should break the ice with a friendly introduction
before discussing the serious problems in the schedule.

43
00:05:09,250 --> 00:05:17,167
The coordinator asks us to figure out how many
volunteers will be available on the opening morning next
week.

44
00:05:17,417 --> 00:05:24,917
We need to make a decision about the venue before the
printer can prepare the invitations for everyone.

45
00:05:25,167 --> 00:05:32,667
Maya will take care of the registration desk while
Daniel checks the microphones in the main meeting room.

46
00:05:32,917 --> 00:05:40,000
Please keep in mind that several visitors use
wheelchairs and need enough space beside the entrance
doors.

47
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:48,167
The new manager hopes we can work together without
repeatedly sending the same documents to different
people by mistake.

48
00:05:48,417 --> 00:05:55,500
I look forward to meeting the volunteers after hearing
so many enthusiastic stories about their previous
projects.

49
00:05:55,750 --> 00:06:03,250
We have to deal with a delayed delivery before it
affects the food supplies for the afternoon session.

50
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:10,583
The receptionist will get in touch with the supplier as
soon as the meeting ends this morning.

51
00:06:10,833 --> 00:06:18,333
Someone should pay attention to the emergency exits when
arranging the chairs around the edges of the room.

52
00:06:18,583 --> 00:06:26,083
A tired volunteer does not want to watch another
demonstration before trying the equipment herself during
the rehearsal.

53
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:34,250
The sign has a blue border and white lettering, and its
measurements are printed clearly beside the technical
drawing.

54
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:42,000
Our visitors can have a go at the activity while an
experienced volunteer explains the instructions beside
them.

55
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:49,750
I ran into an old colleague outside the office, and she
offered to help with the event preparation.

56
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:57,917
We will set up the display after lunch, when the main
room is empty and the floor has dried.

57
00:06:58,167 --> 00:07:05,667
Daniel turned the music down because the speaker beside
the desk was making conversation unnecessarily difficult
for everyone.

58
00:07:05,917 --> 00:07:13,417
Maya looked the address up before writing the directions
in an email to the volunteers from another town.

59
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:21,167
The organizer says no worries when I apologize for
arriving a few minutes later than we originally agreed.

60
00:07:21,417 --> 00:07:28,917
We thank each other for the help and invite the
afternoon volunteers to join the meeting as well.

61
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:37,083
After the event we meet at a quiet cafe, where our
friends are discussing the latest neighborhood news
together.

62
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:44,833
Maya asks me to spill the tea about the surprise party
that everyone apparently organized without telling her.

63
00:07:45,083 --> 00:07:53,417
I tell her the story is true, no cap, because I
personally helped decorate the room before the guests
arrived.

64
00:07:53,667 --> 00:08:01,167
Daniel accidentally sends the photograph to the wrong
group and says my bad when he realizes his mistake.

65
00:08:01,417 --> 00:08:08,917
Maya replies no biggie because the photograph does not
reveal anything that the guests should not see yet.

66
00:08:09,167 --> 00:08:16,667
Nobody wants to throw shade at the organizer, although
the unusual decorations provide plenty of opportunities
for jokes.

67
00:08:16,917 --> 00:08:24,417
Our British friend warns that we should not take the
piss out of someone who seems genuinely upset.

68
00:08:24,667 --> 00:08:32,583
The waiter spilled the tea across the tray, and the hot
liquid soaked several paper napkins beside the cups.

69
00:08:32,833 --> 00:08:40,750
The bottle has no cap on its neck, so the remaining
water could easily spill inside the shopping bag.

70
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,917
A tall tree will throw shade across this terrace in
summer, although there is almost no shade here today.

71
00:08:49,167 --> 00:08:56,667
I ask my friend what's up when she arrives looking
unusually excited about the message on her phone.

72
00:08:56,917 --> 00:09:04,833
We usually hang out here after work because the cafe
remains open later than most shops in the neighborhood.

73
00:09:05,083 --> 00:09:13,000
Daniel needs to chill out after a stressful afternoon,
so he orders a drink and stops checking his messages.

74
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:21,167
We catch up after several months apart, sharing stories
about our families and the projects that kept us busy.

75
00:09:21,417 --> 00:09:28,917
The atmosphere helps us break the ice with the new
member of our group who moved here recently.

76
00:09:29,167 --> 00:09:36,667
I had a go at describing the surprise in English, and my
friends understood almost everything I said.

77
00:09:36,917 --> 00:09:44,833
The menu lists coffee, tea, milk, and three varieties of
cake, with their prices printed beside each item
clearly.

78
00:09:45,083 --> 00:09:53,000
The waiter carries a brown tray to the window table
while another customer searches quietly for a missing
glove.

79
00:09:53,250 --> 00:10:00,750
Nobody does it for a reward, because helping our friends
organize a small celebration is enjoyable by itself.

80
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:08,500
We promise to keep in touch after Maya leaves for her
new job in another city next month.

81
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:17,083
The next morning our friends travel to the coast,
carrying sandwiches and a printed map of the local
walking routes.

82
00:10:17,333 --> 00:10:24,833
We set off early because the road becomes crowded when
visitors leave the city during the weekend holidays.

83
00:10:25,083 --> 00:10:32,583
Daniel asks everyone to watch out for bicycles before
stepping across the narrow street beside the bus
station.

84
00:10:32,833 --> 00:10:40,750
We find out that the first bus has been canceled after
reading a notice beside the closed ticket window.

85
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:48,500
Maya suggests we take a break while another member of
the group searches for a different route online.

86
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:56,667
We must make sure that the replacement bus stops near
the coastal path rather than the distant industrial
harbor.

87
00:10:56,917 --> 00:11:04,833
The driver promises to drop us off beside the museum
after delivering the other passengers to the central
station.

88
00:11:05,083 --> 00:11:13,417
One traveler asks us to look after her small bag while
she purchases a bottle of water from the kiosk.

89
00:11:13,667 --> 00:11:21,167
We run out of clean cups before lunch, so everyone has
to use their own drinking bottle instead.

90
00:11:21,417 --> 00:11:29,333
The fog gradually disappears and we can carry on walking
along the path without worrying about the distant
cliffs.

91
00:11:29,583 --> 00:11:37,917
Our guide tells us to slow down because several younger
children are still climbing the steep steps behind our
group.

92
00:11:38,167 --> 00:11:46,500
I picked a smooth stone up from the sand and examined
the unusual pattern before returning it to the beach.

93
00:11:46,750 --> 00:11:54,667
We came across an old photograph of the village in a
display inside the small museum near the harbor.

94
00:11:54,917 --> 00:12:02,417
The museum opens on a daily basis during summer, but
closes on Mondays throughout the colder winter months.

95
00:12:02,667 --> 00:12:10,583
Visitors can sit down beside the window and read the
information about boats while waiting for the guided
tour.

96
00:12:10,833 --> 00:12:18,333
A visitor tries to watch it in English, but the
documentary only has subtitles in two other languages.

97
00:12:18,583 --> 00:12:26,917
The harbor contains six fishing boats and a yellow
crane, and several workers are painting a wooden fence
beside them.

98
00:12:27,167 --> 00:12:35,083
A handwritten notice describes the measurements of the
bridge and gives the date of its most recent structural
inspection.

99
00:12:35,333 --> 00:12:42,833
Maya says at least the rain has stopped, even though our
afternoon bus will arrive later than expected.

100
00:12:43,083 --> 00:12:51,000
We spend our spare time taking photographs and share the
best pictures with the absent members of our group.

101
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:59,583
Back at home I describe the trip to my sister while she
prepares a simple meal for our visiting grandparents.

102
00:12:59,833 --> 00:13:07,750
She asks me to turn on the kitchen lamp because the
window no longer provides enough light for cooking.

103
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:16,333
We put the plates away after dinner and leave the clean
cups on a towel beside the sink to dry.

104
00:13:16,583 --> 00:13:24,500
My grandfather likes to bring up stories about the
village whenever someone mentions a journey to the coast
nearby.

105
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:32,667
Our grandmother asks everyone to calm down when several
children begin describing their favorite moments at the
same time.

106
00:13:32,917 --> 00:13:40,833
My sister will help out tomorrow if the community
organizers need another person to check the registration
forms upstairs.

107
00:13:41,083 --> 00:13:49,000
I do not want to give up learning English merely because
a difficult conversation makes me feel embarrassed
occasionally.

108
00:13:49,250 --> 00:13:57,167
My grandmother says that small improvements add up over
time, especially when a learner practices regularly and
remains patient.

109
00:13:57,417 --> 00:14:05,333
We can work out a convenient schedule together rather
than repeatedly canceling lessons when our family
responsibilities change unexpectedly.

110
00:14:05,583 --> 00:14:13,500
The children get along well despite having different
interests and frequently disagreeing about which game
they should play next.

111
00:14:13,750 --> 00:14:22,083
I still listen to stories before bed, sometimes using
the same familiar recording that I enjoyed earlier in
the week.

112
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:30,250
We acquire language through repeated contact with
meaningful speech, rather than through memorizing a list
of disconnected grammar rules.

113
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:38,417
There is a lot of useful information in those stories,
including expressions that people use in ordinary daily
conversations.

114
00:14:38,667 --> 00:14:46,167
My sister explains the instructions over and over again
until the children can complete the activity without
assistance.

115
00:14:46,417 --> 00:14:54,750
I now recognize patterns that once seemed confusing, and
familiar recordings no longer require as much deliberate
concentration as before.

116
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:03,333
The exercise makes learning easier for our youngest
students, especially when they can discuss the pictures
with their older classmates.

117
00:15:03,583 --> 00:15:11,917
My sister asks me to forget any detailed travel plans
for tomorrow because the weather forecast predicts heavy
rain overnight.

118
00:15:12,167 --> 00:15:20,500
She says the repair should be easy enough if we have the
correct tools and follow the printed instructions
carefully.

119
00:15:20,750 --> 00:15:29,083
The box contains a small screwdriver, two metal
brackets, and four identical screws wrapped separately
in a transparent plastic packet.

120
00:15:29,333 --> 00:15:37,667
I say see you to our grandparents at the door and
promise to visit again after the workshop next week.

121
00:15:37,917 --> 00:15:46,250
A month later our neighborhood starts a new project, and
unfamiliar volunteers arrive with different expectations
about the work ahead.

122
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:54,000
We need to get the hang of the equipment before allowing
visitors to operate it without close supervision.

123
00:15:54,250 --> 00:16:02,167
The coordinator is down to earth and explains every task
clearly without trying to impress us with technical
language.

124
00:16:02,417 --> 00:16:10,333
I feel under the weather this morning, so another
volunteer offers to handle the announcements while I
rest briefly.

125
00:16:10,583 --> 00:16:18,917
We should not cut corners when checking safety
equipment, even if the inspection takes longer than our
original schedule allowed.

126
00:16:19,167 --> 00:16:25,833
One volunteer accidentally let the cat out of the bag
about the surprise guest during lunch.

127
00:16:26,083 --> 00:16:34,417
The gardener physically cut corners from the square
fabric so that it would fit around the curved flower
container outside.

128
00:16:34,667 --> 00:16:43,417
A builder placed a large model under the weather chart
on the wall, where the two diagrams could be compared
easily.

129
00:16:43,667 --> 00:16:52,000
Our team will go the extra mile to help visitors who
cannot easily read the small print on registration
forms.

130
00:16:52,250 --> 00:17:00,583
We can call it a day after the final equipment check,
because nobody needs to remain in the building
overnight.

131
00:17:00,833 --> 00:17:09,583
I need to take a rain check on dinner because my sister
has asked me to collect her from the station.

132
00:17:09,833 --> 00:17:17,750
The coordinator promises that the registration problem
will be a piece of cake once the missing forms finally
arrive.

133
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:26,333
Her explanation is spot on, and everyone understands why
the visitors were directed to the wrong entrance earlier
that afternoon.

134
00:17:26,583 --> 00:17:34,500
We are finally on the same page about the schedule after
comparing the different messages that everyone received
yesterday.

135
00:17:34,750 --> 00:17:43,917
The editor draws a red circle on the same page as the
photograph because there is no room on the facing page.

136
00:17:44,167 --> 00:17:52,500
A new volunteer admits that she cannot want to watch
another demonstration without trying the equipment
herself during the session.

137
00:17:52,750 --> 00:18:01,500
Several boxes contain unused paper, spare pens, and a
stack of blank labels that the printer delivered
yesterday afternoon to reception.

138
00:18:01,750 --> 00:18:10,500
I do it because the neighborhood benefits from the
project, although the work sometimes interrupts my
ordinary weekend plans at home.

139
00:18:10,750 --> 00:18:19,083
One volunteer gets input from the coordinator before
writing a short announcement about the change to the
afternoon schedule today.

140
00:18:19,333 --> 00:18:28,083
Our visitors exchange photographs of the finished
project while the volunteers close the windows and move
the remaining chairs into storage.

141
00:18:28,333 --> 00:18:37,083
On Friday our group discusses an unexpected message
about the project while waiting for the evening train to
leave the station.

142
00:18:37,333 --> 00:18:45,667
Daniel says that helping the shy visitor find her family
made my day, and he is still smiling about it.

143
00:18:45,917 --> 00:18:54,667
Maya replies that the first public demonstration was a
big deal for someone who had never spoken before a large
audience.

144
00:18:54,917 --> 00:19:03,250
The volunteers hit it off immediately, although they
came from different towns and had never worked together
before that afternoon.

145
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:11,833
One person thinks Daniel is pulling her leg when he
claims that a famous singer will attend the next event.

146
00:19:12,083 --> 00:19:20,833
Maya confirms the message for real, explaining that the
singer personally wrote to the coordinator after
visiting our neighborhood last summer.

147
00:19:21,083 --> 00:19:29,417
Everyone hopes the coordinator can come through with the
final arrangements before the visitors begin making
expensive travel plans online.

148
00:19:29,667 --> 00:19:38,417
The assistant tells us to hold your horses because the
date is still uncertain and the singer has not confirmed
it.

149
00:19:38,667 --> 00:19:47,417
The photographs from the completed project hit different
now that I know how much work the volunteers contributed
behind the scenes.

150
00:19:47,667 --> 00:19:55,583
Daniel promises to give us a heads up when the schedule
changes, rather than announcing it after we arrive.

151
00:19:55,833 --> 00:20:04,167
A maintenance worker physically pulled her leg free from
the gap after the visitor slipped between two wooden
steps nearby.

152
00:20:04,417 --> 00:20:13,167
The guide says that actual horses should remain in their
stalls, and asks the riders to hold your horses there
carefully.

153
00:20:13,417 --> 00:20:22,167
The builder explains that a ball will come through the
opening if someone drops it into the narrow pipe from
above.

154
00:20:22,417 --> 00:20:31,167
I do it after checking the message, because replying to
our coordinator is my ordinary responsibility during the
project this month.

155
00:20:31,417 --> 00:20:40,583
The timetable gives departure times in black lettering
and marks the final service with a small red triangle
beside its destination name.

156
00:20:40,833 --> 00:20:50,000
My phone displays a photograph of the empty station
platform, and the battery indicator shows that only
fourteen percent remains this evening.

157
00:20:50,250 --> 00:20:59,000
The message is really simple and clearly explains where
everyone should wait before entering the building
through the side entrance tomorrow.

158
00:20:59,250 --> 00:21:08,417
We expect to get input from the venue manager before
deciding which rooms will be available to the public
during the event.

159
00:21:08,667 --> 00:21:17,417
The volunteers ask whether the instructions will be easy
enough for visitors who have never used this type of
equipment before.

160
00:21:17,667 --> 00:21:27,250
We close the discussion and quietly watch the final
passengers board the train while a railway employee
checks the doors beside each carriage.

161
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:36,667
Before the next event our team holds a rehearsal, using
unfamiliar examples to check whether the volunteers
understand the instructions without assistance.

162
00:21:36,917 --> 00:21:45,250
The rehearsal went smoothly despite a delayed start, and
the coordinator thanked everyone for remaining patient
during the equipment checks.

163
00:21:45,500 --> 00:21:53,417
We learned the ropes by observing an experienced
volunteer who explained why the usual procedures
sometimes need small adjustments.

164
00:21:53,667 --> 00:22:02,000
A helpful colleague asks us to bear in mind that the
visitors may be nervous about asking questions in
public.

165
00:22:02,250 --> 00:22:10,583
The manager asks everyone to play it by ear if the
singer arrives late and the published schedule becomes
impossible.

166
00:22:10,833 --> 00:22:18,750
One volunteer feels out of the loop because she missed
the meeting where the changed arrangements were
originally explained.

167
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:27,333
Maya promises to fill her in before the event begins,
including the information that was missing from the
earlier email.

168
00:22:27,583 --> 00:22:36,333
Daniel says that our tired coordinator deserves to take
it easy during the weekend after managing so many
unexpected problems recently.

169
00:22:36,583 --> 00:22:45,333
A visitor asks whether we can lend a hand with the heavy
boxes that need to be moved upstairs before opening.

170
00:22:45,583 --> 00:22:53,917
We keep an eye on the entrance while another volunteer
assists the visitors who are waiting beside the
information desk.

171
00:22:54,167 --> 00:23:02,917
The coordinator says it is time to hit the road before
the weather worsens and the evening traffic becomes much
heavier.

172
00:23:03,167 --> 00:23:11,917
A technician tests whether a falling wooden block can
physically hit the road surface without damaging the
painted demonstration model nearby.

173
00:23:12,167 --> 00:23:20,500
The supervisor makes a literal circular diagram and
places a photograph out of the loop that surrounds the
remaining pictures.

174
00:23:20,750 --> 00:23:29,083
A volunteer says she prefers to watch it in English
because the translated version sounds unfamiliar to her
young children.

175
00:23:29,333 --> 00:23:37,667
The technician forgot any elaborate seating plans that
depended on using the room currently occupied by the
unfinished exhibition upstairs.

176
00:23:37,917 --> 00:23:47,500
The chart contains the names of twelve volunteers and
the times when each person will arrive at the building
during the morning tomorrow.

177
00:23:47,750 --> 00:23:56,500
We recognize the contribution of a volunteer who was on
the ball and noticed the missing safety sign before
opening time.

178
00:23:56,750 --> 00:24:05,500
Maya reminds us that experience makes a difference when
unexpected problems appear and there is little time to
consult written instructions.

179
00:24:05,750 --> 00:24:14,083
Nobody should jump to conclusions about a complaint
before listening carefully to the visitor who actually
experienced the difficulty firsthand.

180
00:24:14,333 --> 00:24:23,500
The coordinator thanks the volunteers, closes the
notebook, and checks that every light in the meeting
room has been switched off safely.
